From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:56:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: keep a terminated thread's state on iOS while it may
 still run

ThreadTerm for another thread suspends it, reads its context to wait for
the suspend, and then frees its FEX state and emulator stack. On the iOS
host the suspend does not always stop the thread: the server holds a
thread only at a safe point and otherwise retries on a timer, and
NtGetContextThread does not wait for that. The childabort probe's
terminate phase, built for x86-64, showed it: the first terminated worker
was released from a wait after its state was freed, faulted on its freed
emulator stack, and every thread of the process then spun, so the phase
hung in its first round. WoW64 had the same race (fex 0027).

On the iOS host a thread terminated by another now leaves the registry
and the sweep registry and keeps its state; the server ends it at a safe
point in translated code or at its next block. What a terminated thread
leaks is its FEX state and emulator stack. A thread that terminates itself
is torn down as before, and the CPU area's SuspendDoorbell pointer, which
the server now rings for a killed thread, is cleared before the state it
points into is freed.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-12-killed-threads.md
Offered-upstream: no; the suspend that may not stop a thread is the iOS port's
---
 Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
index 579ef13..2114a8d 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/ARM64EC/Module.cpp
@@ -2362,7 +2362,11 @@ NTSTATUS ThreadTerm(HANDLE Thread, LONG ExitCode) {
     }
 
     Threads.erase(it);
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+    if (StatAllocHandler && Self) {
+#else
     if (StatAllocHandler) {
+#endif
       StatAllocHandler->DeallocateSlot(CPUArea.ThreadState()->ThreadStats);
     }
 #ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
@@ -2378,6 +2382,23 @@ NTSTATUS ThreadTerm(HANDLE Thread, LONG ExitCode) {
    * ABBA loop through a sweeper blocked on a map lock whose holder wants
    * TCM via a memory notify. */
   IosSweepUnregisterThread(ThreadState);
+
+  /* On iOS the suspend above may not have stopped the thread: the server holds a
+   * thread only at a safe point and otherwise retries later, and NtGetContextThread
+   * does not wait for that. A thread still running met its freed state: a fault on
+   * its freed emulator stack, after which every thread of the process spun
+   * (PLA-226, as PLA-223 for WoW64). Its state is left to it: the thread is about
+   * to be killed, and the server ends it at a safe point in translated code or at
+   * its next block (madeira-unix 0127, 0130). What a terminated thread leaks is its
+   * FEX state and emulator stack. */
+  if (!Self) {
+    LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[thr-term] CROSS-TERM tid={:#x}: its FEX state is kept, the thread may still run", ThreadTID);
+    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+  }
+
+  /* The server rings a killed thread's suspend doorbell through this pointer
+   * (madeira-unix 0130); clear it before the state it points into is freed. */
+  CPUArea.Area->SuspendDoorbell = nullptr;
 #endif
 
   delete GetFrontendThreadData(ThreadState);
